  "directions": [
    {
      "step": 1,
      "title": "Prep",
      "text": "Cube pork, salt generously, dry brine if you have time. The sear is the most important step though — you can handle salting later if needed."
    },
    {
      "step": 2,
      "title": "Sear",
      "text": "Sear pork in small batches until well browned. Take care not to scorch the bottom of the pot — you don't want burnt flavors."
    },
    {
      "step": 3,
      "title": "Aromatics",
      "text": "Sautee onion and garlic."
    },
    {
      "step": 4,
      "title": "Tomato Paste",
      "text": "Add tomato paste, cook the funk off."
    },
    {
      "step": 5,
      "title": "Braise",
      "text": "Deglaze with stock. Add chipotles, seared pork, and all spices. Minimize liquid — you don't want it wet."
    },
    {
      "step": 6,
      "title": "Pressure Cook",
      "text": "Pressure cook 90 minutes at high (~11 psi). Natural release — no \"keep warm,\" don't touch the vent."
    },
    {
      "step": 7,
      "title": "Finish",
      "text": "Add lime juice and zest. Consider frying it up when it's done."
    }
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Cubed because you don't want muscle fibers to be weirdly long. Imagine long pulled pork — gross.",
    "If you don't make your own stock or can't get gelatinous stock from the refrigerated section, just use white wine."
  ],
